WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.835
Task statement 1.6.

2
00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:05.979
Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows.

3
00:00:05.979 --> 00:00:16.585
A predictable nightly pipeline and an open-ended outage investigation need opposite decomposition strategies, and the exam asks you to tell them apart.

4
00:00:17.985 --> 00:00:21.053
There are two basic ways to break down a big task.

5
00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:25.471
If you already know the steps, use a fixed chain, like an assembly line.

6
00:00:25.471 --> 00:00:31.607
If the steps only appear as you dig in, you need an adaptive plan, like a detective following clues.

7
00:00:31.607 --> 00:00:35.841
Anthropic's advice is to choose the simplest shape that does the job.

8
00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:41.902
A prompt chain runs known steps in order, with optional checks in between.

9
00:00:41.902 --> 00:00:43.917
Code review is the classic case.

10
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:53.805
Review each file on its own so nothing gets skimmed, then run a separate integration pass that looks across files for broken interfaces and mismatched calls.

11
00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:58.340
One giant pass over many files spreads attention thin and misses things.

12
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.549
Open-ended work is different.

13
00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:06.978
Start by mapping the structure, then find the areas that matter most, then make a plan.

14
00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:09.286
As you learn things, change the plan.

15
00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:14.216
This is where an orchestrator that creates subtasks at run time earns its cost.

16
00:01:14.216 --> 00:01:19.644
If you can list every subtask in advance, a fixed chain is usually simpler and cheaper.

17
00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:27.638
In the CI scenario, a fourteen file pull request gets one review per file and then a cross-file pass.

18
00:01:27.638 --> 00:01:33.513
In the developer productivity scenario, someone asks for tests on a legacy billing module.

19
00:01:33.513 --> 00:01:37.365
The agent maps the modules, ranks the risky ones and plans.

20
00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:42.719
Then it finds an undocumented tax service, and the plan changes to cover it first.

21
00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:45.577
Watch for three traps.

22
00:01:45.577 --> 00:01:51.279
One huge pass over many files is wrong; split it per file and add an integration pass.

23
00:01:51.279 --> 00:01:56.318
A rigid upfront plan for an open investigation is wrong; adapt as you learn.

24
00:01:56.318 --> 00:02:01.953
And reaching for a dynamic orchestrator when the steps are already known is overkill.

25
00:02:01.953 --> 00:02:03.809
The simple chain wins there.

26
00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:05.764
To recap.

27
00:02:05.764 --> 00:02:08.781
Use a fixed chain when the steps are predictable.

28
00:02:08.781 --> 00:02:13.955
Use an adaptive plan when the task is open-ended, starting with a map of the ground.

29
00:02:13.955 --> 00:02:18.389
For code review, go file by file, then check how the files fit together.

30
00:02:18.389 --> 00:02:22.639
And always start with the simplest structure that solves the problem.

